akr 0.1
project akr

record akr.work.a-scope-that-can-never-match/1 : work {
    title "AKR-G022 and AKR-G023 are silent on most of what they were built to catch"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-core/src/**",
        path "docs/05-validation-rules.md",
        path "docs/10-freshness-and-git.md",
        path "spec/diagnostics/**"
    ]
    intent """
        A scope or watch glob that matches no tracked file makes its record unfalsifiable: nothing the
        glob names can ever change, so the record can never go stale and never enter the review queue.
        AKR 0.5.0 already has the right diagnostics for this — AKR-G022 for a watch glob and AKR-G023
        for a scope path, with V-102's help text saying exactly the right thing: "the record can no
        longer go stale by that glob". They are not the problem. The problem is that in a nine-agent
        cleanup run across fifteen workspaces on 2026-09-08, not one agent saw either of them fire until
        one was provoked deliberately in a throwaway workspace, while a hand scan of the same corpus
        found 93 distinct dead globs.

        THE CENSUS. A hand scan using `git ls-files -- ':(glob)<pattern>'` over every top-level scope
        and watches block in every ledger under /mnt/Samsung980_1TB/Rust-projects, deliberately not
        using AKR's own diagnostic: 93 distinct dead globs out of 1142 examined, across the 13
        workspaces that have records and globs to test, cited by 254 record-occurrences. By
        classification: 119 deleted or never-existed, 96 gitignored, 32 untracked-but-present, 7
        wrong-shape. By kind: 214 scope, 40 watches. Dead rates per workspace range from 0% (Kitchen-
        Concept, Thai-alphabet-app, openarc) to 27.6% (bpg-rs), with SaveYourSkin the largest ledger and
        16.7%. This ledger is not exempt: 2 of AKR's own 73 globs are dead, and they happen to be one
        instance of each suppression below.

        SUPPRESSION 1 — the diagnostic stops once the record is completed. Proved in a throwaway
        workspace: a work record with two zero-match scope paths raised two AKR-G023 warnings; running
        `akr complete` on it, changing nothing else, made both vanish, and re-reading the record file
        confirmed both dead paths were still there. The census measures the cost: 88 of the 254
        occurrences, 35% of the corpus, sit on `completed` records. The freshness rationale does not
        transfer. A completed record's scope is a durable claim about what that work touched, and it is
        read by future agents long after completion — the whole reason this cleanup run existed was
        that people trust the ledger's account of finished work. Silencing it buys a quiet queue at the
        cost of the ledger's historical accuracy.

        SUPPRESSION 2 — the diagnostic is suppressed for a git-ignored path and fires for a merely
        untracked one. This was the confusing one, because it looked like the diagnostic firing at
        random. It is an exact rule, confirmed in four workspaces with `git check-ignore -v` and with no
        exceptions:
          - FreeCAD, one record, four globs, three dead, only two reported: `FreeCAD/` is matched by
            .gitignore:25 and is SILENT; `blwfish-freecad-mcp` and `yonosoft-freecad-mcp` are untracked
            but not ignored and both FIRE. The apparent "a deeper glob escaped its shallower sibling"
            had nothing to do with depth.
          - SaveYourSkin: SYSEngine/assets/characters/{garments,human} exist on disk, are untracked and
            are not ignored, and FIRE — its own papercut calls this permanent noise on a correct scope.
          - Lege-ecosystem: lege-document-ocr/turboocr/** is matched by lege-document-ocr/.gitignore:3
            and is SILENT.
          - A deliberate probe: one gitignored directory that exists on disk, scoped five ways at once
            (a plain file path, dir/sub/**, dir/**, a bare directory, a bare subdirectory). All five
            silent. It is not glob shape, not depth, not file-versus-directory. It is ignore status.

        The rule gets both ends backwards. A git-ignored path is the one that can NEVER match a tracked
        file — the permanently unfalsifiable case, the one worth shouting about — and it is silenced: 96
        of 254 occurrences. Untracked-but-not-ignored is the genuinely ambiguous case, and it is
        reported: 32 occurrences, which include both real signal (a FreeCAD constraint requiring a
        COMMITTED codegraph.json exclude list, where the file exists with the right content and was
        simply never `git add`ed — silencing that would hide an unmet requirement) and pure noise
        (SaveYourSkin's deliberately-untracked art tree). AKR is inferring authorial intent from
        .gitignore and landing on the wrong side of both.

        A THIRD WAY A SCOPE DIES, which no predicate above catches and which looks perfectly healthy to
        a human reviewer: bpg-rs scopes `libbpg/**`, and libbpg is a git submodule. The parent repo
        tracks the gitlink but never the submodule's contents, so `ls-files` never expands into it. Any
        ls-files-based fix has to decide what it does at a submodule boundary.

        A FOURTH THING, separate from dead globs but found by the same scan and belonging with the
        validation work: jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase4e-modular-effort-ramp/1's scope contains
        double-wrapped glob strings — the stored value is literally the text `path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-
        cli/**"`, quotes and keyword included. AKR parsed it, stored it, and never objected. Malformed
        input accepted silently.

        WHAT THE NEXT SESSION MUST DECIDE.

        1. Whether a completed record's scope is still a claim. If it is, G023 must keep reporting after
        completion, and 88 occurrences appear at once. If it is not, say so in the docs, because every
        agent in this run assumed it was.

        2. Stop inferring intent from .gitignore. Make the predicate `git ls-files` for every scope path
        and watch glob — which is what AKR-G023's own message already promises, "matches no tracked
        path" — and give the author a way to say "this path is deliberately untracked". That is the one
        thing SaveYourSkin's papercut says it cannot express, and it is what separates a glob that
        matches nothing because the work is not done yet (signal) from one that can never match (noise).
        A naive "warn on every zero-match glob" flattens that distinction and will be turned off.

        3. Decide the submodule case explicitly rather than by accident.

        4. Reject a scope path that does not parse as a bare glob.

        One thing this record does NOT claim, because it was checked and is false: the importer is not
        the cause. `akr import` and `akr ingest` set neither `watches` nor `scope` on anything they
        draft — neither string appears in either module. Every dead glob in the census was written by an
        author, not emitted by a tool, which is why the fix has to be a check rather than a generator
        change.
        """
    note """
        LANDED 2026-09-08, all four parts, with tests: `asserts_its_scope()` replacing the `is_live()`
        filter so a completed record's scope is still checked and a sealed superseded one is not;
        `unverifiable_scopes()` reporting ignored and submodule paths as build facts;
        `Repository::run_ls_tree_submodules()` and `inside_submodule()` for the gitlink case; and
        `validate()` in freshness/glob.rs rejecting a glob containing a quote or beginning with the
        slot keyword, with a malformed scope glob now raising AKR-G021 where it used to be skipped in
        silence. AKR-G023's help no longer advertises the copied-prefix case, because AKR-G021 owns it
        now.

        The build-fact shape was the design call and it is worth stating why, because a warning was the
        obvious choice and would have been wrong. Diagnostic severity here is Error or Warning only,
        and `--strict` is the default, so any new warning would have failed the check of every project
        whose scopes are correct and deliberately outside git — SaveYourSkin's art trees above all,
        whose own papercut calls the existing noise unfixable-without-falsifying-a-correct-scope. A
        fact reports without failing, which is the shape staleness and scratch already have (D-024,
        D-036).

        NOT CLOSED BY THIS RECORD: there is still no way for an author to say "this path exists and is
        deliberately untracked". Ignored paths are now visible as facts, which removes the silence, but
        an untracked-and-not-ignored path still warns and still cannot be declared. That is tracked
        separately; it is a grammar question about `project.akr` rather than a matching-predicate
        question, and it is the one piece of this cluster that is a design call rather than a bug.
        """
    acceptance {
        check completed-records-are-reported {
            statement """
                A completed record's dead scope raises AKR-G023; a superseded record's does not, because a sealed record cannot be edited to fix it.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-core --test freshness a_completed_records_dead_scope_is_still_reported && cargo test -p akr-core --test freshness a_superseded_records_dead_scope_is_not_reported"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.scope-verifiability-suite-20260908/2 ]
        }
        check malformed-globs-say-so {
            statement """
                A scope glob carrying the slot keyword in its value, or a quoted string nested inside another, raises AKR-G021 rather than being silently skipped and later reported as a dead scope.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-core --test freshness a_malformed_scope_glob_is_g021_and_not_a_dead_scope"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.scope-verifiability-suite-20260908/2 ]
        }
        check unverifiable-scopes-are-facts {
            statement """
                A scope path the repository ignores, or one inside a submodule, is reported as a build fact rather than as a diagnostic, and appears in `akr check`'s build-facts block.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-core --test freshness an_ignored_scope_is_a_build_fact_rather_than_a_diagnostic && cargo test -p akr-core --lib submodule"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.scope-verifiability-suite-20260908/2 ]
        }
    }
}

record akr.work.a-scope-that-can-never-match/2 : work {
    title "AKR-G022 and AKR-G023 are silent on most of what they were built to catch"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-core/src/**",
        path "docs/05-validation-rules.md",
        path "docs/10-freshness-and-git.md",
        path "spec/diagnostics/**"
    ]
    intent """
