record akr.work.errors-that-withhold-what-akr-already-knows/1 : work {
    title "Two errors that withhold what akr already knows: the MCP name and the workspace one level down"
    state completed
    scope [ path "AGENTS.md", path "crates/akr-cli/src/**", path "spec/diagnostics/**" ]
    intent """
        Two failures that every agent in the 2026-09-08 nine-agent run hit, both one-line fixes, both
        cases where the tool has the answer and prints something else.

        ONE — an MCP tool name is not recognised as an MCP tool name.
        `akr knowledge.context` answers `error[AKR-C001]: unknown command "knowledge.context"`, with no
        help line. So does `akr knowledge_context`. AKR's own AGENTS.md lines 13-27 open with exactly
        the right sentence — "`knowledge.*` names are MCP tools, not CLI commands" — and then give the
        full mapping table: knowledge.context to `akr context --goal`, knowledge.start to `akr start`,
        knowledge.propose to `akr propose --from`, and so on. The mapping is written down. It is written
        down in the one place the people who need it are not: an agent working in bpg-rs or
        Evidence-intake-project reads that project's guidance, or a global instruction file that spells
        the operation `knowledge.context`, and never opens AKR's AGENTS.md. Papercuts in bpg-rs and
        LegeOS both record an agent's first required context call simply failing for this reason.
        Fix: when the unknown command matches `knowledge[._]<name>` and `<name>` resolves to a real
        command, say so and print the equivalent invocation. `akr check --help` already does the
        equivalent thing well for one case — "`validate` is the same command under the name the MCP tool
        uses" — so the precedent and the tone already exist.

        TWO — "no .akr directory" is true and useless when fifteen of them are one level down.
        Run from /mnt/Samsung980_1TB/Rust-projects, `akr check` answers `error[AKR-C011]: no .akr
        directory found in /mnt/Samsung980_1TB/Rust-projects or any parent`, help: "run `akr init` to
        create one". That parent holds fifteen AKR workspaces and is not itself a git repository, so the
        suggested remedy is actively wrong: `akr init` there would scaffold a ledger in a directory that
        can never have git facts. This is how the entire nine-agent run was configured — the MCP server
        was rooted at that parent, every `knowledge.*` call answered "no .akr directory", and every agent
        independently fell back to driving the CLI with `cd` or `--dir`. An orderflower papercut records
        the same discovery being made a second time.
        Fix: on AKR-C011, scan one level of subdirectories for `.akr` and, if any are found, say so —
        "no workspace here; found 15 in subdirectories: ..." — and suppress the `akr init` suggestion
        when the current directory is not a git repository, since a workspace without git facts is not
        what the author wants.

        Neither of these is a design question. Both are the same shape: the tool performed a lookup, the
        lookup failed, and the failure message reported the miss instead of the information the tool
        already had in hand.
        """
    note """
        LANDED 2026-09-08. `mcp_equivalent()` in args.rs maps `knowledge.` and `knowledge_` names onto
        their CLI commands, including the `evidence_*`, `source_*` and `handoff_*` families and
        `validate` to `check`, and falls through to the existing edit-distance suggestion otherwise.
        `workspaces_below()` in session.rs scans one level — one deliberately, since this answers "you
        are one directory too high" rather than searching a filesystem — and AKR-C011's help names what
        it found. Where nothing is below, the `akr init` suggestion is now conditional on `.git`
        existing; outside a repository it says so instead of inviting a workspace that can never have
        git facts.

        The part worth keeping is what the first half turned out to be. The mapping was never missing:
        AGENTS.md carries the whole table and opens by saying "`knowledge.*` names are MCP tools, not
        CLI commands". It is written down in the one place the people who need it are not — an agent
        working in another project reads that project's guidance, or a global instruction file that
        spells the operation `knowledge.context`, and never opens this repository's AGENTS.md. Two
        ledgers carry a papercut about the resulting round trip. Documentation that only reaches people
        who already know where to look is not documentation, and the fix was to say it at the point of
        failure, where the tool already had the answer.
        """
    acceptance {
        check mcp-names-are-recognised {
            statement """
                `akr knowledge.context`, `akr knowledge_evidence_add` and `akr knowledge.validate` each name their CLI equivalent in the help line, and an ordinary typo still gets the edit-distance suggestion.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.six-point-of-failure-fixes-20260908/1 ]
        }
        check the-workspaces-below-are-named {
            statement """
                AKR-C011 from a directory holding AKR workspaces names how many are one level down and lists them, and outside a git repository it stops suggesting a ledger that could have no git facts.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.six-point-of-failure-fixes-20260908/1 ]
        }
    }
}

record akr.work.four-guidance-gaps-answered/1 : work {
    title "Four guidance gaps that cost agent time in every session, answered in the documents agents read"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "AGENTS.md",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/validate/**",
        path "docs/05-validation-rules.md",
        path "docs/DECISIONS.md",
        path "spec/diagnostics/codes-lang.md"
    ]
    intent """
        Four things this workspace's agents had to work out for themselves, repeatedly, because nothing
        wrote them down. Each is now answered where an agent reads rather than where a maintainer
        remembers.

        THE NORMATIVE LAYER AT `proposed`, and why it was stuck. Answered by D-042, recorded in
        docs/DECISIONS.md and mirrored as @akr.decision.proposed-means-recorded-and-an-observation-is-support.
        `proposed` on a normative record means recorded, not pending; and V-021 now accepts an
        observation as support, because that is the citation an author reaches for first and refusing
        it silently is what manufactured the permanently-proposed layer measured in
        @akr.observation.the-normative-layer-is-stuck-at-proposed. One entry added to CITEABLE in
        validate/rules.rs, message and help rewritten to name the kinds that count, and two tests.

        A MOTIVATING OBSERVATION WHOSE DEFECT IS FIXED. AKR-R021 refuses to supersede an observation an
        active decision is `supported_by`, and it is right to: that observation is the decision's
        rationale, and retiring it would leave the decision resting on nothing. But the observation has
        stopped being a claim about current code, so re-observing it at HEAD would be false. The answer
        is neither: it has become history, and history should not be watched. Give it `watches [ ]` and
        a `review_after` and leave it `verified`. The tool guarded two agents into this answer today
        with no way for either to know it was the answer; it is now in AGENTS.md.

        A CLAIM ABOUT THE WORLD OUTSIDE THE REPOSITORY. A library's behaviour, what a registry
        publishes, what an operating system ships: no glob can see any of it change. `watches` is the
        wrong instrument and leaving it empty is correct; `review_after` is the lever, and the statement
        should say what would falsify it. Named in AGENTS.md with the failure mode agent-pack hit — a
        watch on `Cargo.toml` standing in for "no crate does X" goes stale on every unrelated dependency
        bump and stays silent on the only change that matters.

        `--about` ON A PAPERCUT. It is what makes a papercut findable by the project that can fix it;
        without it `akr papercut collate` cannot route it and it stays where it was written. Of 395
        papercuts across fifteen workspaces on 2026-09-08, 278 carried no subject at all. Now stated in
        the papercut section of AGENTS.md rather than left to be inferred from the collate help.
        """
    acceptance {
        check the-decision-is-recorded {
            statement """
                D-042 is in docs/DECISIONS.md with its Honored by list, V-021's statement in docs/05-validation-rules.md and AKR-R031's row in spec/diagnostics/codes-lang.md name `observation`, and AGENTS.md carries all four pieces of guidance.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.v021-accepts-an-observation-20260908/1 ]
        }
        check v021-accepts-an-observation {
            statement """
                An active decision citing only the observation that motivated it validates; one citing only another decision still raises AKR-R031.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-core --test v_rules v021"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.v021-accepts-an-observation-20260908/1 ]
        }
    }
}

record akr.work.handoff-context-transport/1 : work {
    title "Generalise the advisor packet into an agent-to-agent context transport"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "AGENTS.md",
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/handoff/**",
        path "crates/akr-mcp/src/**",
        path "docs/17-handoff.md",
        path "scripts/agent-section.md"
    ]
    intent """
        Make moving work between agents one mechanism rather than three. The advisor packet of the previous wave was a special case wearing the name of the general thing: an advisor, a scout, a worker and a reviewer differ in exactly one respect, how much of the parent's judgement the child may see, and that is a field rather than a subsystem. Delivered as three inherited levels resolved by reference (a derived project capsule, a session capsule holding the user's request verbatim, and packets in four modes), results filed as packets, a coverage roll-up so the next wave is delegated against evidence rather than a hunch, fourteen knowledge.handoff_* MCP tools, and an akr start notice that sends a child to its packet instead of re-deriving the project.
        """
    note """
        Also collapses the agent-facing guidance from four files to one, at four lines: three command lines, a ten-word reason, and a pointer. Recorded as D-041. Note that @akr.work.advisor-packet-handoff/1's acceptance checks name a test file this work renamed; they are honest about what was verified at the commit they were observed at, and are left alone rather than rewritten.
        """
    acceptance {
        check coverage-names-the-gap {
            statement """
                Coverage aggregates across results and reports what some child named as unopened and no child read.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-cli --test handoff coverage_aggregates_and_names_what_nobody_opened"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.handoff-transport-suite/1 ]
        }
        check disclosure-follows-the-mode {
            statement """
                worker discloses the parent's notes on an ordinary open; scout, reviewer and advisor withhold them until a recorded reveal.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-cli --test handoff disclosure_follows_the_mode"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.handoff-transport-suite/1 ]
        }
        check guidance-is-one-place {
            statement """
                The agent-facing handoff brief exists in scripts/agent-section.md alone, at four lines, with no second copy in AGENTS.md, a skill file, or the MCP document beyond its verbatim embed.
                """
            method manual
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.handoff-guidance-single-source/1 ]
        }
        check inheritance-by-reference {
            statement """
                A packet inherits the session and project capsules rather than copying them, so correcting a capsule corrects every packet cut from it.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-cli --test handoff a_packet_inherits_the_session_rather_than_copying_it"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.handoff-transport-suite/1 ]
        }
        check narrowing-stays-visible {
            statement """
                The session's request is carried verbatim and a narrowed assignment renders beneath it rather than in place of it.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-cli --test handoff the_session_request_is_carried_verbatim_and_a_narrowing_stays_visible"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.handoff-transport-suite/1 ]
        }
        check suite-and-design-set-hold {
            statement """
                The whole test suite passes and the design set still coheres with D-041, docs/17-handoff.md and AKR-C044 in it.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test && python tools/check-design.py"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.handoff-design-set/1 ]
        }
        check surfaces-agree {
            statement """
                knowledge.handoff_open and akr handoff open produce an identical result object, opening over MCP does not stamp the packet, and coverage matches from either side.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-mcp --test differential a_handoff_opens_the_same_way_from_either_surface"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.handoff-transport-suite/1 ]
        }
    }
    author "GitHub Uploader"
}

record akr.work.handoff-packet-fingerprint-and-result-inheritance/1 : work {
    title "Fingerprint each packet at the cut and inherit predecessors' filed coverage"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/handoff/**",
        path "crates/akr-mcp/src/**",
        path "docs/17-handoff.md",
        path "scripts/agent-section.md"
    ]
    intent """
        Close the two implementation misses an outside review found in the handoff transport before it is relied on. First, drift was measured from the session's fingerprint, captured when the delegation began, so a parent's own preparatory edits read to the child as drift against a packet cut later; a packet now carries the fingerprint of the tree it was cut against, and open, expand and verify compare against that (session show still measures from the session). Second, inheritance rendered only the preparer's notes stored in the parent packet, not the coverage the agent who received that packet later filed in its result; resolution is now transitive and cycle-safe and follows each inherited packet to its results, with what they read, searched, ran and changed shown to every mode and their findings following the mode's disclosure rule, revealed through the same recorded door as the notes. Alongside: the four-line brief said the mode commands 'invoke' agents when AKR builds a packet and launches nothing; the handoff_open structured content duplicated the whole briefing so both halves counted against one budget, and is now an index into the text; the project capsule's content-addressed id is recomputed when boundaries change; the MCP overflow hint named sections that did not exist.
        """
    note """
        Advice reviewed against the code before acting; all four findings held. Deliberately not done, per the same advice: no new modes, no orchestration inside AKR, no summarisation or compaction, and the project capsule stays a single mutable file rather than a per-digest store.
        """
    acceptance {
        check drift-is-per-packet {
            statement """
                A tree edited after the session opened but before the packet was cut is exact for the packet and drifted for the session; an edit after the cut is drift for the packet.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-cli --test handoff drift_is_measured_from_the_packet_not_the_session"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.handoff-refinement-suite/1 ]
        }
        check inheritance-is-transitive-and-cycle-safe {
            statement """
                C inheriting B inheriting A receives A's coverage, and a cycle renders each predecessor once.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-cli --test handoff inheritance_is_transitive_and_survives_a_cycle"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.handoff-refinement-suite/1 ]
        }
        check open-does-not-duplicate-itself {
            statement """
                knowledge.handoff_open and akr --format json handoff open return the same structured index, which carries neither the request nor the session head, while the text still carries the verbatim request and the project capsule.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-mcp --test differential a_handoff_opens_the_same_way_from_either_surface"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.handoff-open-index-differential/1 ]
        }
        check result-coverage-is-inherited-mode-aware {
            statement """
                A successor sees a predecessor's filed coverage in every mode; the predecessor's findings reach a worker on open and the other modes only through a recorded reveal.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p akr-cli --test handoff a_successor_inherits_what_its_predecessor_actually_read"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.handoff-refinement-suite/1 ]
        }
    }
    derived_from [ @akr.work.handoff-context-transport/1 ]
    author "GitHub Uploader"
}

record akr.work.import-drafts-land-as-live-work/1 : work {
    title "akr import writes 125 live work items and never mentions akr ingest"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "crates/akr-cli/src/import.rs",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/import/**",
        path "crates/akr-core/src/ingest/**",
        path "docs/12-migration.md"
    ]
    intent """
        Importing five discursive planning documents into the Lege-ecosystem ledger in 2026-08 produced
        125 live `proposed` work records and a 1291-line ACTIVE-WORK.md that no one could read. Clearing
        it on 2026-09-08 took 125 separate `akr abandon` calls. The records were not wrong so much as
        not work: "Executive verdict", "Wall times (median of 5 runs)", "Peak RSS, single representative
        run", "Reproduce", "Audit limitations", "1. Objective", "6. Quick reference", and negative
        guidance like "Do not start with" and "Two traps — do NOT fix these by loosening a check".

        THE MECHANISM, read in the source rather than inferred from the output.
        `import::extract_markdown` emits one claim per heading, unconditionally, skipping only an empty
        title and a body-less level-1 heading when other headings exist. `import::classify` is five
        keyword rules and a catch-all, and the catch-all is `work`. Its own comment gives the rationale:
        "everything else is `work` — the state a durable claim of unknown kind is safest in, because a
        `proposed` work record asserts nothing normative." That is true about normativity and false
        about the work queue. A proposed work record is rendered into ACTIVE-WORK.md and reads as a live
        commitment; safety from asserting a norm is not safety from filling the backlog.

        TWO THINGS THIS RECORD DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CLAIM, because both were checked and are false.
        First, the importer does not mis-attach body text: the excerpt is the section body's first
        paragraph, taken verbatim by slicing, and the case that looked wrong is faithful — "## 3. Add
        `Jp2DecodeStats`" genuinely opens with "Do not start by optimizing `MqDecoder`...". A discursive
        document's first paragraph is simply a poor summary of its own heading, and the module's tests
        correctly pin verbatim slicing. Second, the importer is not the source of the mis-shaped watch
        globs seen in bulk-ported ledgers: neither `watches` nor `scope` appears anywhere in
        import/ or ingest/.

        THE SHARP PART. AKR already ships the review gate this needed, and `import` does not mention it.
        `akr ingest` is preview, start, show, `mark <candidate> <disposition>`, apply, close — a
        per-candidate review workflow. Nothing in `akr import --help` names it, distinguishes which
        document shape each is for, or warns what a discursive document will produce. The Lege-ecosystem
        port did not hit a missing feature; it used the wrong one of two, with no signpost.

        THREE CANDIDATE ANSWERS, in increasing order of what they repair:

        (a) `akr import --help` and docs/12-migration.md name `ingest` and say which document shape each
        is for. One paragraph, fixes the next port only.

        (b) `import` warns, or refuses without an explicit flag, above a threshold of drafted claims,
        naming `ingest`. Small, and the threshold is a judgement to make rather than a number to guess:
        the five documents here drafted between 7 and 61 claims each.

        (c) The one that repairs the ledgers already in the ground: ACTIVE-WORK excludes a `proposed`
        work record whose only source is `kind legacy` and which has never been revised. That is an
        undispositioned import draft, not live work. It is a view-level predicate needing no schema
        change, and every one of the 125 would have been invisible until someone actually adopted it —
        which is exactly what `import`'s tracking record (D-022) already asks a reviewer to do.

        (c) is the recommendation. It also composes with (a): once drafts are not live, `import` becomes
        a reasonable default again and `ingest` is the path for a document you want to disposition
        interactively.
        """
    note """
        ATTEMPTED AND REVERTED 2026-09-08, and the failure is the useful part of this record.

        Option (c) was implemented as a predicate on ACTIVE-WORK's `live_work`: hide a work record that
        is `proposed`, carries only a `legacy` source, and is at revision 1 — the reasoning being that
        any of those three ceasing to hold is somebody adopting the draft. It compiled, its unit test
        passed, and it is wrong.

        A `proposed` record is edited IN PLACE (D-015: only a sealed record needs a new revision). So a
        draft that somebody has read, rewritten and adopted as the project's live plan is still at
        revision 1, still `proposed`, and still cites only the document it came from. The predicate
        would have hidden exactly such a record: during this same cleanup,
        lege-ecosystem.work.jp2lam-jpeg-2000-decoder-optimization-plan was revised into the single live
        record for that whole effort — what landed, the negative results, the seven open items — and it
        remains proposed at revision 1 with one legacy source. Hiding it would have buried the most
        useful record in that ledger while leaving the 125 phantoms it replaced untouched, had they not
        already been abandoned.

        Nor do the obvious refinements hold. Acceptance blocks and relations look like adoption signals
        and are not: that record has neither, and plenty of genuine drafts would acquire a `part_of`
        edge from the importer itself. Nothing structural on the record distinguishes an adopted draft
        from an unadopted one, because in-place editing leaves no trace.

        WHERE THE SIGNAL ACTUALLY IS, and this is what the next session should implement. `akr import`
        already maintains a tracking work record whose acceptance checks enumerate the claims and
        demand each be "dispositioned: promoted as <key> or declined with evidence" (D-022). A draft is
        undispositioned exactly while its tracking check is unsatisfied — which is a fact the ledger
        already holds, computed by the same acceptance machinery every view uses, and which becomes
        false the moment a human or agent actually dispositions the claim. That is the correct
        predicate: ACTIVE-WORK hides a work record that some live import tracking record still lists as
        undispositioned. It costs a cross-record lookup that `live_work` does not currently do, which is
        why it was not landed on the way past.

        The general lesson is worth more than the fix: a state machine that edits in place cannot be
        asked "has anyone touched this?", and a predicate that assumes it can will be confidently wrong.
        """
}

record akr.work.init-gitignore-is-never-reconciled/1 : work {
    title "akr init's .gitignore block is written once and never reconciled"
    state completed
    scope [ path "crates/akr-cli/src/init.rs", path "crates/akr-core/src/**" ]
    intent """
        `akr init` writes a .gitignore block, and GITIGNORE_ENTRIES (crates/akr-cli/src/init.rs:77) now
        holds three entries: `.akr/cache/`, `.agent/scratch/` and `.agent/handoffs/`. A workspace
        initialised before `.agent/handoffs/` was added keeps the two-entry block forever, because
        nothing reconciles it. Checked on 2026-09-08: this repository has all three; LegeOS,
        Lege-ecosystem, bpg-rs and jpegXL-rs have only the first two, and LegeOS is carrying 49
        untracked files under .agent/handoffs as a result. They are permanently untracked, permanently
        noisy in `git status`, and permanently one `git add -A` away from being committed by accident.

        The question of whether handoff packets are ephemeral or durable provenance is already answered
        — init ignores them — so this is not a design call. It is that the answer only reaches
        workspaces created after it was made.

        The fix is small and there are two shapes of it. Either `akr check` warns when the workspace's
        .gitignore is missing an entry the current binary's init would write, which puts the
        reconciliation where every session already looks; or `akr init` is made idempotent on an
        existing workspace so re-running it tops up the block, which is what a user would guess it does
        anyway. The first is better, because nobody re-runs init on a workspace that already works.

        Worth checking the same way whenever GITIGNORE_ENTRIES changes again: the list is a piece of
        project setup that AKR authors on the project's behalf, so AKR owns keeping it true.
        """
    note """
        LANDED 2026-09-08. `missing_gitignore_entries()` in init.rs compares the workspace's .gitignore
        against GITIGNORE_ENTRIES, and `akr check` prints the difference in its build-facts block.
        Verified against LegeOS, which predates `.agent/handoffs/` and is carrying 49 untracked packets
        because of it.

        A build fact rather than a warning, deliberately and for the same reason as the
        unverifiable-scope line: `--strict` is the default, and failing the check of four out of five
        existing workspaces over a .gitignore line is how checks get turned off.

        Reporting from `check` rather than making `init` idempotent is the whole point. Nobody re-runs
        `init` on a workspace that already works, so a top-up command would never be run; the
        reconciliation has to happen where every session already looks. The general shape is worth
        keeping in view — the .gitignore block is project setup AKR authors on the project's behalf,
        written once at creation and never revisited, which is the same shape as the generated views
        being emitted once and never re-checked. Anything AKR writes on a project's behalf needs an
        answer to "and what keeps it true afterwards".
        """
    acceptance {
        check the-missing-entry-is-reported {
            statement """
                `akr check` in a workspace whose .gitignore predates an entry `akr init` now writes names the missing entry as a build fact, without failing the check.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.six-point-of-failure-fixes-20260908/1 ]
        }
    }
}

