record akr.work.retire-the-already-answered-papercuts/1 : work {
    title "Retire the reported AKR defects that current behaviour has already answered"
    state completed
    scope [ path "crates/**", path "docs/**" ]
    intent """
        A defect register compiled from fifteen workspaces on 2026-09-08 carried ten items that turned
        out to need no fix. Seven were fixed by work already shipped and nobody had gone back to say so;
        three were never defects. Left unmarked, each would have been re-investigated by the next agent
        to read the papercut, which is how a register becomes a tax rather than an asset — and one of
        them, "freshness rebuilds hang for minutes", would have actively deterred people from using AKR
        on large ledgers for something it never did.

        Every one was re-run rather than assumed. The results are in
        @akr.evidence.papercut-retest-20260908: the descendant-commit gate closed by D-028 and D-029,
        `akr validate` existing, `akr get` showing acceptance ids, `akr revise --from` merging rather
        than replacing, `evidence add` then `complete` needing no build between them, the concurrent
        write race closed by the workspace lock, and the 0.1.0 stub renderers all present. Plus three
        that were correct behaviour or misattribution: `akr get --format json` timing, `topic` on a work
        record, and the freshness-rebuild hangs.

        The concurrent-write one is worth keeping in view rather than merely closing. Six simultaneous
        writers used to leave two records; the reproduction now leaves six. This cleanup run was nine
        agents writing to fifteen workspaces in parallel, which is exactly the condition that defect
        described, so the fix was load-bearing for the run that found it.

        The general point, and the reason this record exists at all rather than ten quiet state changes:
        a papercut that has been fixed and not retired is indistinguishable from one that is still live.
        Four of these ten were being cited as live problems in this run's own working notes. `akr
        papercut collate` gathers them into the owning project, which is the right mechanism, but
        nothing prompts the owning project to re-test what it absorbed. Whoever runs collate next should
        expect a meaningful fraction to be already-fixed and should say so per entry rather than
        absorbing them all as live friction.
        """
    acceptance {
        check re-tested-not-assumed {
            statement """
                Each of the ten was re-run against akr 0.5.0 — in the reporting workspace where the reproduction needed it, in a throwaway workspace where it did not — and the outcome recorded per item.
                """
            method command
            command "see @akr.evidence.papercut-retest-20260908 for the per-item reproductions"
            verified_by [ @akr.evidence.papercut-retest-20260908/1 ]
        }
    }
}

